 "9efadbd73ca147ec8dbad29ea1e63adba0869968e326d557c12a02d3005bd9ef": {
  "content": "Debian and Ubuntu are both Linux distributions, but they differ mainly in their release philosophy, target audience, and default software choices:\n\n| Aspect | Debian | Ubuntu |\n|--------|--------|--------|\n| **Release cycle** | Long‑term stable releases every ~2 years; testing/unstable branches for newer packages. | Regular releases every 6 months; LTS (Long‑Term Support) every 2 years with 5‑year support. |\n| **Target users** | Developers, sysadmins, and users who want a highly stable, configurable system. | Desktop users, developers, and enterprises looking for a ready‑to‑use system with frequent updates. |\n| **Package management** | Uses `apt` with `.deb` packages; default repository is the “stable” branch. | Uses `apt` too, but adds its own repositories (e.g., `universe`, `multiverse`) and Snap/Flatpak support. |\n| **Software selection** | Very conservative; packages are frozen at release time. | More up‑to‑date; includes newer drivers, desktop environments, and proprietary drivers. |\n| **Community vs. Canonical** | Community‑driven, no single corporate owner. | Developed by Canonical Ltd., with commercial support options. |\n| **Desktop experience** | Minimal default install; users choose their own desktop environment. | Comes with GNOME (or other flavors) pre‑installed, with a polished user interface. |\n\nIn short, Debian prioritises stability and freedom, while Ubuntu focuses on ease of use, frequent updates, and commercial backing.",
  "tokens_in": 1692,
  "tokens_out": 372,
  "tool_calls": []
 },
